# Did reality match what an imagined step predicted? As an ordinary program.
#
# This was `workbench.deviates`, and it was one of the three predicates the capability audit decomposed.
# It got the shortest answer of the three: it is a failed cast and nothing else, so all it ever needed
# was a way to ask *how* a cast failed rather than *whether* it did.
#
# `check` insists, `is_a` answers yes-or-no, and neither could say how — which is the enforcing-form-
# before-answering-form shape this project keeps finding. The `violations` native completes the trio by
# handing back the answer as a node.
#
# Deviation is a failed cast rather than a subgraph diff, and that is why it is cheap: the transformation
# already records the type it promised, so checking it is bounded and already written. Comparing whole
# subgraphs would be expensive and noisy, and irrelevant differences would swamp the real ones. The
# expected type is the honest signal because it is exactly the promise the function made.
#
# The wider, concrete check — *did the files actually appear* — is a different question with a different
# owner: `predicted_changes` derives it from the two frames and `unmet_expectations` tests it. This one
# asks only about the promise.

# The violations of `result` against what `transformation` promised. A node with ordered `violation`
# edges; nothing on it means reality matched.
#
# A transformation that promised nothing cannot be disappointed, so it answers an empty set rather than
# refusing: `expects` is absent whenever the function declares no return type, which is ordinary.
fn deviates(transformation, result) -> thing:
    ATTR R(expected) F(transformation) "expects"
    JMPIF R(expected) .cast
    NEW R(result) "violations"
    JMP .end

    # **Nothing came back where a node was promised**, which is a deviation and not an error: a call can
    # return no result at all, and a plan that promised a `listing` has then been disappointed in the
    # plainest possible way. Said here rather than by the native, because the native takes a node and the
    # instruction set refuses to pass it nothing — an operand that holds nothing is how this graph tells
    # *no part* from *a part that is nothing*, and that refusal is worth keeping rather than relaxing for
    # one caller. The phrasing matches `types.fails`, which is where the Python answered it.
.cast:
    JMPIF F(result) .real
    NEW R(result) "violations"
    NEW R(v) "violation"
    SET R(v) "about" "<node>"
    SET R(v) "expected" "a node"
    SET R(v) "actual" "None"
    LINK R(result) "violation" R(v)
    JMP .end
.real:
    NATIVE R(result) "violations" F(result) R(expected)
.end:
    CONST R(done) true
